  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "The comparisons cannot isolate the distinct-source gate: include an ungated directed-transition control before attributing improvements to repeated support. Objective-only SASRec remains a credible winner.",
    "Source reliability n[a]/(n[a]+tau) does not measure reliability of edge a→b. Rare destinations can receive clipped positive lift from singleton edges, and two distinct sources need not constitute independent evidence.",
    "Training transition counts include the edges being predicted. This is permitted training-data fitting, but can inflate training support and margins; prefix-masking diagnostics cannot detect this shortcut.",
    "The fixed TF–IDF comparison is underspecified: replacing learned text vectors requires explicit pooling, normalization, amplitude calibration, and artifact definitions. Its scale must be comparable with the directed residual.",
    "Sparse lookup, full-catalog scoring, per-category budget allocation, and the packed-feature contract remain unspecified. Packing integer item IDs into floating-point features requires explicit dtype and precision handling.",
    "Originality 4 is defensible as a provisional assessment. Future potential 7 lacks coverage evidence, and code difficulty 4 may underestimate efficient causal scoring and predictor integration. Validation uncertainty and adaptive selection limitations should also be reported."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Investigate whether positive log-lift and source-level reliability amplify rare-edge noise, and identify an appropriate ungated control.",
      "query": "In sequential recommendation using directed item-transition counts, what evidence compares positive PMI or smoothed log-lift with edge-count shrinkage, distinct-user support, and minimum-support filtering? Include sparse Amazon datasets and controls separating transition scoring from multiple-source gating."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Clarify the distinction between legitimate fitted training statistics and optimistic training diagnostics caused by each target contributing its own transition evidence.",
      "query": "For sequential recommenders with global item-transition graphs fitted exclusively on training histories, how do studies assess target-edge memorization using leave-one-user-out or cross-fitted graph diagnostics? Distinguish in-sample optimism from validation leakage and discuss computationally inexpensive approaches."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Assess implementation feasibility and calibrate the proposed residual against a fully specified fixed lexical alternative.",
      "query": "What efficient sparse implementations support SASRec additive item-transition residuals at every causal training position with sampled candidates and at full-catalog inference? Compare CSR lookup or sorted edge-key lookup with sparse TF-IDF history pooling, including memory complexity, bounded-score calibration, and ungated versus support-gated ablations."
    }
  ]
